{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Bring him down to me, that I may set my eye upon him.</b> This is difficult: what difference does it make that he said they should bring him down to him and that his intention was to set the eye of his providence upon him? And further, it is difficult that we do not find that Yosef ever said 'I will set my eye upon him.' To be sure, regarding what they said, 'My lord asked his servants, saying, Have you a father or a brother?' (Genesis 44:19) — even though we do not find that Yosef asked them about this, nevertheless from the response we learn what was on the mind of the questioner. For since they were compelled to defend themselves against the accusation of 'You are spies' (Genesis 42:9) and to say 'We have an old father' (Genesis 44:20), and as they said, 'And the youngest is with our father this day' (Genesis 42:13), it seemed to them as though he had asked them about this explicitly — for surely you know that we are not spies, but you came to investigate through this whether we have a father or a brother; therefore 'And we said to my lord' and so on — because we understood your intention and on that basis we answered; we hid nothing. But the statement 'I will set my eye upon him' — we do not find that Yosef said this at all, even by hint." "And it appears to explain that this 'setting of the eye' is not the setting of the eye of his providence, but rather because Yosef said to them that through Binyamin he wished to test their words, as it is written, 'By this you shall be tested' (Genesis 42:15) — and if so, his intention was to set his eyes upon him and to see through him whether their words were truthful or not. And the commentators said that at first it was stated there, 'And your words shall be tested' (Genesis 42:16), and after they were in prison he said to them, 'And your words shall be verified' (Genesis 42:20). For testing means inquiry and investigation, like examining a minor casually, and the brothers did not agree to this, for they said, 'Lest they teach him to speak lies,' and they chose to sit in prison. And afterward he took them out of prison and said to them, 'And your words shall be verified,' without inquiry and investigation, and to this they agreed to bring him. And therefore he said to him here: you said 'I will set my eye upon him' (Genesis 42:34), for merely by bringing him you will see that our words are very much verified — so how have you suddenly turned upon him, to roll about and fall upon us?"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:26.548870968Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}